  "body": "On main since PR #1058 merged (2026-11-12). Found by our high-tier review of PR #1058 (review comment of 2026-11-12 on that PR, head ef57929b); the head that merged (5f689447) added only merges of main and a test-fixture fix on top, so the finding is in main.\n\n**What is wrong.** `crates/app/src/setup_workholding.rs`, `program_problems`: a setup's stated workholding (chuck / collet) maximum rpm is the limit for the spindle that turns the WORK. The check uses `spindle_law::turns`, which also counts live-tool operations - the driven tool's own spindle. The review's local test: a mill-turn cross drill at 10000 rpm on a setup with a 100 rpm chuck limit is refused with \"turns the work at 10000 rpm\", although the work does not turn at that speed (C is positioned or turns slowly) and the tool's spindle is not held by the chuck.\n\n**Why P1.** A legal program is refused (the post is blocked); nothing unsafe is posted. A user with a mill-turn and a stated chuck limit cannot post live-tool work.\n\n**Expected.** The chuck limit judges only the speed at which the work spindle turns: G97 turning sections (refused above it), the G96 clamp (capped by it), and for a live-tool section the C/main-spindle speed if the work turns during it - never the live tool's rpm. If both spindles run in one section, each is judged against its own limit.\n\nDone means: the cross drill above posts; a G97 turning op above the limit is still refused by name and the G96 clamp is still the lowest of machine, tool and chuck; a live-tool section during which the work spindle itself turns above the limit is refused; pinned in `lathe_workholding_rpm` (or its sibling) with both cases.\n",
